  <div class="section-label">Phase 3 — Weber County Segment</div>
  <h2>1800 North to 4000 South (West Haven)</h2>

  <p>The Phase 3 segment runs from 1800 North in West Point south to 4000 South in West Haven — the portion currently under State Environmental Study.{% cite "udot-ses-schedule" %} Local officials have indicated UDOT is studying an alignment along 5100 West; UDOT has not published a preferred alternative.{% cite "whc-warnke-minutes" %} Until UDOT publishes formal alignment maps in the Draft State Environmental Study (expected Fall 2026), no right-of-way boundary has been formally drawn for any parcel in this segment.</p>

  <p>UDOT's stated standard footprint is <strong>250 feet</strong>, designed to accommodate four travel lanes separated by a median and flanked by multi-use trail corridors.{% cite "udot-ses-build" %} Applied to a 5100 West centerline, that footprint would extend 125 feet to each side of the road. Properties within roughly 500 feet of the eventual centerline will be evaluated for noise impacts; properties within the 250-foot right-of-way boundary would be subject to acquisition once the project is funded.</p>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <p><strong>Current status:</strong> No preferred alignment has been officially selected as of April 2026. The State Environmental Study is in the Alternatives Development and Evaluation phase. No specific parcel data has been publicly released. Property impacts will not be determinable until UDOT publishes the Draft State Environmental Study with formal right-of-way boundaries.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-label">Phase 4 — Long-Range Segment</div>
  <h2>4000 South to Box Elder County Line</h2>

  <p>The segment south of 4000 South extending toward the Box Elder County line is in the long-range planning window under the Wasatch Front Regional Council 2023–2050 Regional Transportation Plan (2043–2050 phase).{% cite "wfrc-rtp-2023-2050" %} No State Environmental Study has been initiated for this segment, and no alignment, footprint, or design has been determined. Phase 4 planning will begin in a future study cycle; the source record does not support further specificity.</p>

  <div class="section-label">Residential &amp; Agricultural Displacement Data</div>
  <h2>Davis County Phase 2 — Closest Available Analog</h2>

  <p>No Weber County parcel-level impact data has been published. The Phase 2 Davis County re-evaluation (2024–2025){% cite "udot-wdc-phase2-noise" %} provides the best available analog: design refinements between the 2017 Environmental Impact Statement and the updated study expanded the project footprint, increasing impacts across all resource categories.</p>

